# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, and auditing our own pre-registration

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Most robot-learning results are shown in simulation, on costly arms or on data-centre GPUs. We wanted to know which of them hold on hardware a student can buy, so we re-tested the central claim of {{papers}} papers on a low-cost SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX. Each re-test ran the cheapest baseline first and had its pass mark fixed before the run. Of the {{c_paper}} claims taken from the papers, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail, and the rest are not proven or not tested. Three proposed classes cover the failures we saw: methods that miss a real-time budget on the edge GPU, zero-shot judges that a trivial signal beats, and latent planners that the commanded joint positions beat. We audited the study itself as well. Every published number was rebuilt from its stored rows, the early gates were recomputed with one statistic, and commit order shows which bars came before their results. Most did, but for {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates the order cannot be shown. The class assignment waits for a blind second reader. All compute cost ${{cost}}.

## 1. The register

A claim shown on a Franka-class arm, in simulation or on a data-centre GPU does not tell you whether it holds on the hardware most students and small labs own. Ours is that kind of hardware: an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB, €{{rig_eur}} for the parts with a stated price ({{rig_unpriced}} small parts are not priced yet). Few of the papers we re-tested were shown on such hardware. {{rig_sim}} of the {{papers}} were shown in simulation and {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k, against {{rig_5to20k}} at $5–20k and {{rig_lt5k}} below $5k; {{rig_unstated}} does not state its setup.

The register has one entry per paper. It records the claim as the paper states it, the pass mark we fixed for our rig, our result and a verdict, and every number in it can be recomputed from published rows. Two rules govern each entry. Before a learned model gets credit, the trivial signal it must beat (copy-last, the commanded joints, episode length) runs on the same rows. And the pass mark is committed before the result, a rule whose record §2 audits.

We picked the papers for relevance to the stack this rig runs: latent world models, VLA policies, failure monitors and judges, action chunking and edge latency. Each went into the register before its test. They are not a sample of the field (§5), and the register file already lists the papers scheduled next.

## 2. Method, and an audit of our own pre-registration

Each gate of the experiment log has a bar and a result. For each, we find in git history the first commit that contains the bar and the first that contains the result, and classify the gate by commit ancestry alone (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`). Anyone with a clone can check ancestry, which is not true of a note or a timestamp, so we use neither. Of the {{pr_rows}} gate rows in the log on 2026-10-31, **{{pr_bar_first}} are bar-first** and {{pr_result_first}} result-first. The others are {{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar and {{pr_not_run}} with a bar but no result yet. The log has since grown to {{pr_today}} rows ({{pt_bar_first}} bar-first, {{pt_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pt_result_first}} result-first, {{pt_no_bar}} no-bar, {{pt_not_run}} not-run). Same-commit means the bar and the result first appear in one commit of this repository, which says nothing about which came first.

Our first pass searched the text of the log and got a different picture: {{v1_bar_first}} bar-first, {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit, {{v1_no_bar}} no-bar and {{v1_unresolved}} unresolved. The log often received a bar and its result in one commit even when the bar had been written earlier, in a protocol, plan, scorer or specification. Tracing each same-commit gate to that file moved {{sc_to_bf}} to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar, because their bar cell declares none, and left {{sc_stay}}. Each upgrade rests on a bar text found in an earlier commit of a file tied to the gate, and the audit table gives the evidence line so a reader can check it. We did not re-audit the rows that were already bar-first for a result hidden elsewhere in the history. One gate added on 2026-11-01, the reward-model progress test, reads same-commit by text search although its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) predates the log commit. We have not audited its result commit, so it stays same-commit as an open candidate.

The early gates mixed point estimates with assorted intervals. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from stored rows with one statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI), and found {{rc_flips}} verdicts whose bar falls inside the interval. The judge accuracy is the clearest case: {{acc_est}} % on {{acc_n}} episodes against a bar of {{acc_bar}} %, a {{acc_verdict}} on the point estimate that an interval of {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % cannot settle. The oscillation rate among {{osc_n}} failed episodes is a {{osc_verdict}} on the point ({{osc_est}} % against a bar of {{osc_bar}} %) with a lower bound of {{osc_lo}} %. A judge threshold's false-intervention rate, {{tau_est}} % on {{tau_n}} episodes against a ceiling of {{tau_bar}} %, is a {{tau_verdict}} whose interval reaches {{tau_hi}} %, and since the threshold was fitted to meet that rate, the interval is only descriptive. The register keeps the point verdicts and marks these three as fragile.

Every published number was recomputed from its published rows, and all {{l0}} of {{l0}} write-ups pass. Rerunning cold is harder. From a fresh clone with no credentials, {{l1_repro}} gates rerun and reproduce, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. Another {{l1_env}} are blocked by one software environment we never published, and the remaining {{l1_skip}} need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint, the arm or a step the write-up leaves out. We also checked the register's citations and paper-side fields against the papers' PDFs and merged the fixes. Of the {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids the published register names, those missing from our papers mirror belong to scheduled tests. One tested paper has no arXiv id and was not checked against its PDF.

