Table 1 lists the register. Counted by paper, {{p_reproduces}} of the {{papers}} reproduce and {{p_fail}} fail, while {{p_not_proven}} are not proven, {{p_not_tested}} was not tested and {{p_withdrawn}} was withdrawn. The register holds {{claims}} claim rows, {{claims_tested}} of them tested. {{c_ours}} of these rows test our own baselines rather than a paper's claim, for example "the commanded joints beat the world model", and {{c_ours_repro}} of those reproduce. On the {{c_paper}} claims taken from papers, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail. Over all rows the count is {{c_reproduces}} reproduce, {{c_fail}} fail, {{c_not_proven}} not proven, {{c_not_tested}} not tested and {{c_withdrawn}} withdrawn.

## 4. Three failure classes

We propose three classes for the {{fail_rows}} failed claim rows, named after what beat the method. In class A it is the clock: the method may work, but on the edge GPU it misses the real-time budget the claim needs. An adaptive chunking method, for example, takes {{ex_a_ours}} per decision against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}. Class B is zero-shot judges, models used without training on this rig to judge success, failure or progress, that lose to a trivial signal. A learned reward model detects failures at AUROC {{ex_b_ours}}, where episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. Class C is learned latents used to plan, rank or track progress that do worse than the commanded joints or a copy-last or action-only baseline. A latent world model ranks the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, below its bar of {{ex_c_bar}} and below the {{ex_c_base}} of an action-only baseline.

These three examples illustrate the classes and count nothing. Under the protocol committed before any reading, per-row labels count only once a blind second reader, labelling the same {{fail_rows}} rows on the same form, agrees with the first pass at Cohen's κ ≥ {{kappa_bar}}. The protocol and the form are in the repository, and the second reading has not happened yet.

## 5. Limitations

Everything here comes from one rig, one arm and one operator, so a failure is a finding about this rig and not a refutation of the paper in its own setting. One person labelled the verdicts, at rest and not always blind to the arm; a second labeller would show whether any verdict flips, and the class check of §4, the only independent reading planned, has not run. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates ({{pr_same_commit}} same-commit, {{pr_no_bar}} with no bar) this repository cannot show that the bar came first, and the {{sc_to_bf}} upgrades of §2 depend on matching a bar text. Under the bootstrap, {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile, and only {{l1_repro}} gates have been rerun cold. The papers were chosen for relevance to one stack, so the reproduction rate describes this register and not the field.

## 6. Related work

Work on the same arm evaluates policies. A standardized real-world benchmark reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881], and a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool decouples VLA inference from 14 simulation benchmarks through a WebSocket protocol and Docker isolation [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family empirically [@2610.18869]. We test something else: the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency) against bars fixed before the run, with our own pre-registration audited. The closest statement of our thesis [@2610.40026] scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, among them reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records, seeds), and its authors state that it is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig instead.

## 7. Conclusion

On a €{{rig_eur}} rig, {{c_paper_repro}} of {{c_paper}} tested paper claims reproduce under bars fixed for this hardware before the run. Compute was cheap, ${{cost}} for all of it. The hard part was order, keeping every bar ahead of its result, and our own history has {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates where we cannot prove we did. The next papers are scheduled in the register file, and every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`).

